---
import Article from "../../../layouts/Article.astro";
import KeyTakeaway from "../../../components/KeyTakeaway.astro";
import InfoBox from "../../../components/InfoBox.astro";
import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Is Agricola Worth It in 2026? An Honest Review"
  description="Nearly twenty years old and still brutal. Does it deserve shelf space today?"
  game="agricola"
  gameTitle="Agricola"
  category="Buying Guide"
  product="agricola-resource-tokens"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Agricola came out in 2007, spent years at the top of every strategy list, and
    invented the template that dozens of worker placement games have used since.
    The question isn't whether it's good. It's whether a game this old and this
    unforgiving still earns its place when the alternatives are so much friendlier.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Yes, if you want a genuinely demanding euro that punishes bad planning and rewards mastery. No, if you want a relaxing evening or you're introducing anyone to the hobby." />

  <div class="my-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <QuickFact label="Players" value="1–4" icon="👥" />
    <QuickFact label="Time" value="90–150 min" icon="⏱️" />
    <QuickFact label="Weight" value="Heavy" icon="🧠" />
    <QuickFact label="First published" value="2007" icon="📅" />
  </div>

  <h2>The Verdict at a Glance</h2>

  <div class="my-6 grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <div class="p-5 bg-emerald-50 rounded-xl border border-emerald-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-emerald-800 mb-3">✅ Buy It If...</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-emerald-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• You want a game with real difficulty and a skill ceiling</li>
        <li>• Tight, resource-starved puzzles are your idea of fun</li>
        <li>• You have a group that plays the same game repeatedly</li>
        <li>• You want enormous variety from Occupation and Improvement cards</li>
        <li>• You'd play it solo — the solo campaign is excellent</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-xl border border-red-200">
      <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-red-800 mb-3">❌ Skip It If...</h4>
      <ul class="text-sm text-red-700/70 space-y-2">
        <li>• You're teaching someone their first heavy game</li>
        <li>• Your group dislikes blocking and take-that tension</li>
        <li>• Losing badly while learning would put you off</li>
        <li>• You want something you can play in an hour</li>
        <li>• You already own a Rosenberg farming game you like</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What It Gets Right</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The scarcity is perfectly tuned</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">You never have enough actions, enough food, or enough reed. Every single turn is a real decision between things you genuinely need, and almost twenty years on very few games have matched how tight this feels.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Family growth is a beautiful mechanism</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">More workers means more actions but also more mouths. It's a single decision that captures the whole game's tension, and it's been copied endlessly since precisely because it works so well.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">STRENGTH</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The card variety is genuinely enormous</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Hundreds of Occupation and Minor Improvement cards mean your hand is different every game and demands a different plan. If you're the kind of player who wants a game to last a hundred plays, this delivers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What It Gets Wrong</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WEAKNESS</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The learning curve is a cliff</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Your first game will go badly and there's no gentle version of that experience. Begging cards feel like a punishment for not knowing something you had no way to know. Some people find that motivating; plenty just don't come back.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WEAKNESS</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">Card draw can be lopsided</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">In the full game, the Occupations and Improvements you're dealt vary hugely in power. Between strong players who know the cards, the deal matters more than anyone wants to admit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">WEAKNESS</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">It's a long game with a lot of fiddling</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Dozens of little wooden bits, a board that grows each round, and a scoring pass at the end that takes real time. Two hours of Agricola is genuinely two and a half by the time everything's away.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Which version should you buy?">
    The revised edition is the one to get: cleaner iconography, better components,
    and a rulebook that actually explains itself. Play the family variant first —
    without Occupations and Minor Improvements — and only add the cards once the
    farm-building puzzle feels natural.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Agricola vs the Alternatives</h2>

  <p>
    If Agricola's tension appeals but the cruelty doesn't, Uwe Rosenberg wrote
    gentler games afterwards that keep the farm-building satisfaction with far
    less starvation. If you want worker placement with a friendlier face and
    prettier cards, <a href="/games/everdell/">Everdell</a> covers similar ground
    at half the weight.
  </p>

  <p>
    But nothing quite replaces Agricola if what you actually want is a game that
    fights you.
  </p>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    Agricola is still one of the best strategy games ever designed, and it is
    still not a game you should give to a beginner. The scarcity is exquisite, the
    decisions are genuinely hard, and mastering it takes a dozen plays.
  </p>

  <p>
    Buy it if that sentence sounds like a promise. Avoid it if it sounds like a
    warning.
  </p>
</Article>
